# WindSurf Rules for Our Rust Project

Welcome to our Rust project! This guide helps everyone stay on the same page about how we work together. Think of it as our shared handbook for collaboration.

## How We Organize Our Files

Our project has a clear structure:
- Put all source code in the `src/` folder
- Keep tests in the `tests/` folder
- Share examples in the `examples/` folder
- Store benchmarks in the `benches/` folder
- Maintain documentation in the `docs/` folder
- Use `Cargo.toml` for dependencies and project info
- Let `Cargo.lock` track exact dependency versions
- Explain the project in `README.md`
- Include license details in the `LICENSE` file
- Store GitHub workflows in `.github/`
- Document all public functions and types
- And of course, you're reading the `.windsurfrules` file now!

## How We Code
- Follow the official Rust Style Guide which are idiomatic, clear, and concise
- Follow Clean Code principles
- Make sure to changes other similar code if doing refactoring
- Make the code simple, do not overcomplicate things

## How We Name Our Branches

When creating a new branch, please name it based on what you're working on:
- For new features: `feature/123-add-cool-thing` (where 123 is the issue number)
- For bug fixes: `bugfix/123-fix-that-problem`
- For urgent fixes: `hotfix/123-critical-fix`
- For releases: `release/v1.2.3`

## How We Write Commit Messages

Please format your commit messages like this:

```
[type]: What you did in a short phrase

More details about what you did and why, if needed.

Issue: #123
```

For the type, choose one of these:
- `feat` when adding something new
- `fix` when fixing a bug
- `refactor` when improving existing code
- `test` when adding tests
- `docs` when updating documentation
- `chore` for routine tasks
- `perf` for performance improvements
- `style` for formatting and style changes
- `ci` for changes to our CI/CD pipeline

## How We Write Code

We believe in writing clean, maintainable Rust code:
1. Follow the official Rust Style Guide
2. Always run `cargo fmt` before committing
3. Make sure your code passes `cargo clippy` checks
4. Document all public functions and types
5. Use these naming conventions:
   - `snake_case` for variables, functions, and files
   - `UpperCamelCase` for types, traits, and enums
   - `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for constants
6. Use Rust expressive type system and derive traits when it makes sense
7. Write idiomatic Rust - embrace the languages patterns

## How We Test Our Code

Good testing makes for reliable software:
1. Write tests for all public functions
2. Aim to cover at least 80% of the code
3. Include integration tests for key features
4. Run tests with `cargo test`
5. Write clear test descriptions
6. Remember to test edge cases

## How We Handle Pull Requests

When contributing code:
1. Create a properly named branch
2. Make your changes with good commit messages
3. Run `cargo test` to make sure everything works
4. Clean up your code with `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy`
5. Update any relevant documentation
6. Open a pull request with a clear description
7. Link to any related issues
8. Wait for the CI checks to pass
9. Ask for a review from a team member
10. Address any feedback you receive
11. A maintainer will merge your code when it is ready

## How We Release New Versions

When it is time for a new release:
1. Create a release branch like `release/v1.2.3`
2. Update the version number in `Cargo.toml`
3. Update the `CHANGELOG.md` with what is new
4. Create a pull request for the release
5. After merging, tag the release on GitHub
6. Publish to crates.io if appropriate

## How We Handle Dependencies

Dependencies need careful management:
1. Keep dependencies up to date
2. Regularly check for security issues with `cargo audit`
3. Do not add dependencies unless they are really needed
4. Choose well-maintained crates
5. Use semantic versioning in your dependency specifications

## How We Think About Performance

Rust excels at performance when used well:
1. Use Rust zero-cost abstractions
2. Be mindful of memory allocations
3. Benchmark critical code with `cargo bench`
4. Choose appropriate data structures
5. Consider async/await for I/O operations

## How We Approach Security

Security matters in every project:
1. Follow OWASP security guidelines
2. Run `cargo audit` regularly
3. Avoid `unsafe` code when possible
4. If you must use `unsafe`, explain why in comments
5. Always validate user input

## How We Document Our Work

Good documentation helps everyone:
1. Use rustdoc comments for all public APIs
2. Include examples in your documentation
3. Keep the README updated and helpful
4. Document changes in the CHANGELOG
5. Write clearly and concisely

## How We Version Our Software

We follow Semantic Versioning:
- Bump the MAJOR version when making incompatible API changes
- Bump the MINOR version when adding features compatibly
- Bump the PATCH version when fixing bugs compatibly

## Custom Command
- "CC" : means commit with context by changes (new file and modified file). If there are mre than one context, make it as multiple commit with individual context

